---
title: Networks and Hierarchies
description: Choose tidy trees, Sankey flows, spatial adjacency graphs, and force-directed networks for connected or nested data.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Network and hierarchy charts show relationships rather than values on two\nindependent quantitative axes. Node-link layouts can produce semantic\ncoordinates for ordinary marks. Area layouts such as treemaps and radial\npartitions such as sunbursts, and weighted flows such as Sankey diagrams,\ninstead depend on final layout bounds and render through responsive composite\nmarks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use these views only when topology is the question. Dense networks quickly\nbecome less legible than a matrix, grouped summary, or searchable table."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-topology","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the topology"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What is the parent-child structure and depth?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tidy hierarchy tree"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which positioned observations are spatial neighbors?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Delaunay adjacency network"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which dependency clusters emerge without fixed positions?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Force-directed network"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does quantity split and recombine?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Sankey"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does value move through staged subtotals?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sankey flow diagram"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How large are branches within a strict hierarchy?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Treemap"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does branch value divide across hierarchy depth?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sunburst"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Must many entities be compared by attributes, not connections?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A table, facets, or quantitative chart"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layout, traversal, grouping, and collision handling belong to eager data\npreparation unless they depend on final chart bounds. TanStack Charts provides\noptional static tree and force transforms plus final-layout spatial, hierarchy,\nand Sankey marks. "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales and D3"}]},{"type":"text","value":" documents that\nboundary."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"start-with-a-basic-sankey","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with a basic Sankey"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The smallest useful Sankey shows a single input splitting into two paths and\nrecombining into one output. Link width is the only quantitative encoding in\nthis example; nodes and links use the chart theme, and every node gets one\nshort name."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use this version as the starting point when the structure matters more than\nstyling. Its four explicit links preserve a total flow of 10 through a 60/40\nsplit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The definition supplies semantic rows and composes ordinary marks after the\nresponsive layout resolves:"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"basic-sankey-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"basic-sankey","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart, link, rect, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { sankeyDiagram } from '@tanstack/charts/network/sankey'\\nimport { links, nodes } from './data'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    sankeyDiagram({\\n      nodes,\\n      links,\\n      nodeKey: 'id',\\n      source: 'source',\\n      target: 'target',\\n      value: 'value',\\n      align: 'left',\\n      nodePadding: 28,\\n      inset: { left: 16, right: 16, top: 24, bottom: 12 },\\n      marks: ({ nodes: layoutNodes, links: layoutLinks }) =>\\n        [\\n          link(layoutLinks, {\\n            x1: 'x1',\\n            y1: 'y1',\\n            x2: 'x2',\\n            y2: 'y2',\\n            key: 'key',\\n            strokeWidth: (flow) => flow.width,\\n          }),\\n          rect(layoutNodes, {\\n            x1: 'x0',\\n            x2: 'x1',\\n            y1: 'y0',\\n            y2: 'y1',\\n            key: 'key',\\n            inset: 0,\\n          }),\\n          text(layoutNodes, {\\n            x: 'x',\\n            y: (node) => node.y0 - 8,\\n            text: (node) => node.data.label,\\n            key: 'key',\\n            fill: 'currentColor',\\n            fontSize: 12,\\n            fontWeight: 650,\\n          }),\\n        ] as const,\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: null,\\n    y: null,\\n  },\\n  guides: false,\\n  margin: 0,\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export const nodes = [\\n  { id: 'input', label: 'Input' },\\n  { id: 'path-a', label: 'Path A' },\\n  { id: 'path-b', label: 'Path B' },\\n  { id: 'output', label: 'Output' },\\n]\\n\\nexport const links = [\\n  { source: 'input', target: 'path-a', value: 6 },\\n  { source: 'input', target: 'path-b', value: 4 },\\n  { source: 'path-a', target: 'output', value: 6 },\\n  { source: 'path-b', target: 'output', value: 4 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, link, rect, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { sankeyDiagram } from '@tanstack/charts/network/sankey'\nimport { links, nodes } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    sankeyDiagram({\n      nodes,\n      links,\n      nodeKey: 'id',\n      source: 'source',\n      target: 'target',\n      value: 'value',\n      align: 'left',\n      nodePadding: 28,\n      inset: { left: 16, right: 16, top: 24, bottom: 12 },\n      marks: ({ nodes: layoutNodes, links: layoutLinks }) =>\n        [\n          link(layoutLinks, {\n            x1: 'x1',\n            y1: 'y1',\n            x2: 'x2',\n            y2: 'y2',\n            key: 'key',\n            strokeWidth: (flow) => flow.width,\n          }),\n          rect(layoutNodes, {\n            x1: 'x0',\n            x2: 'x1',\n            y1: 'y0',\n            y2: 'y1',\n            key: 'key',\n            inset: 0,\n          }),\n          text(layoutNodes, {\n            x: 'x',\n            y: (node) => node.y0 - 8,\n            text: (node) => node.data.label,\n            key: 'key',\n            fill: 'currentColor',\n            fontSize: 12,\n            fontWeight: 650,\n          }),\n        ] as const,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  guides: false,\n  margin: 0,\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=basic-sankey env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export const nodes = [\n  { id: 'input', label: 'Input' },\n  { id: 'path-a', label: 'Path A' },\n  { id: 'path-b', label: 'Path B' },\n  { id: 'output', label: 'Output' },\n]\n\nexport const links = [\n  { source: 'input', target: 'path-a', value: 6 },\n  { source: 'input', target: 'path-b', value: 4 },\n  { source: 'path-a', target: 'output', value: 6 },\n  { source: 'path-b', target: 'output', value: 4 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=basic-sankey file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/111-basic-sankey/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the interactive basic Sankey catalog example"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"customize-a-sankey","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Customize a Sankey"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Sankey diagram makes conservation and decomposition visible at the same\ntime: link width carries quantity, while each node marks a meaningful subtotal\nor outcome. This Apple FY22 income statement follows product and service\nrevenue through gross profit, operating costs, operating profit, and net\nprofit."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"111-sankey-flow","height":"500"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sankeyDiagram"},{"type":"text","value":" owns the responsive flow layout, D3 mutation isolation,\nendpoint resolution, proportional widths, identity, and source lineage. Its\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"marks"},{"type":"text","value":" callback keeps the income statement's authored order, compact wording,\nlabel side, two-line values, backdrops, title, and semantic colors beside the\nnative "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"link"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":" definitions. The application does not import\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-sankey"},{"type":"text","value":"; the exact Charts subpath keeps it out of unrelated consumers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep every intermediate subtotal balanced. Use direct labels and tone as well\nas color so profit and cost paths remain identifiable. See the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/sankey.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sankey diagram reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for responsive layout\noptions and immutable node/link fields."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"show-a-strict-hierarchy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show a strict hierarchy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A tidy tree assigns one position per node and one link per parent-child\nrelationship. Direct labels make a small hierarchy readable without requiring\nhover."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the exact optional transform for a static tidy tree:"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"hierarchy-tree-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"hierarchy-tree","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart, dot, link, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { treeLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { rows } from './data'\\n\\nconst hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\\n  path: 'name',\\n  delimiter: '.',\\n})\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    link(hierarchy.links, {\\n      x1: 'x1',\\n      y1: 'y1',\\n      x2: 'x2',\\n      y2: 'y2',\\n      key: 'id',\\n      stroke: '#94a3b8',\\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\\n    }),\\n    dot(hierarchy.nodes, {\\n      x: 'x',\\n      y: 'y',\\n      key: 'id',\\n      fill: '#2563eb',\\n      r: 4,\\n    }),\\n    text(hierarchy.nodes, {\\n      x: 'x',\\n      y: 'y',\\n      text: 'name',\\n      key: 'id',\\n      fill: '#2563eb',\\n      anchor: (node) => (node.internal ? 'end' : 'start'),\\n      dx: (node) => (node.internal ? -7 : 7),\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear },\\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\\n  },\\n\\n  guides: false,\\n  margin: { top: 24, right: 110, bottom: 24, left: 64 },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export const rows = [\\n  { name: 'Product' },\\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics' },\\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics.Reports' },\\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics.Dashboards' },\\n  { name: 'Product.Platform' },\\n  { name: 'Product.Platform.API' },\\n  { name: 'Product.Platform.Workers' },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, dot, link, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { treeLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nconst hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\n  path: 'name',\n  delimiter: '.',\n})\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    link(hierarchy.links, {\n      x1: 'x1',\n      y1: 'y1',\n      x2: 'x2',\n      y2: 'y2',\n      key: 'id',\n      stroke: '#94a3b8',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    dot(hierarchy.nodes, {\n      x: 'x',\n      y: 'y',\n      key: 'id',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      r: 4,\n    }),\n    text(hierarchy.nodes, {\n      x: 'x',\n      y: 'y',\n      text: 'name',\n      key: 'id',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      anchor: (node) => (node.internal ? 'end' : 'start'),\n      dx: (node) => (node.internal ? -7 : 7),\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  guides: false,\n  margin: { top: 24, right: 110, bottom: 24, left: 64 },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=hierarchy-tree env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export const rows = [\n  { name: 'Product' },\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics' },\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics.Reports' },\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics.Dashboards' },\n  { name: 'Product.Platform' },\n  { name: 'Product.Platform.API' },\n  { name: 'Product.Platform.Workers' },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=hierarchy-tree file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/36-hierarchy-tree/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the larger Flare hierarchy catalog example"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parentId"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"path"},{"type":"text","value":" for explicit parent-reference rows.\nPath input may omit ancestors; the result includes those structural nodes with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data: null"},{"type":"text","value":" and empty source lineage. Explicit rows retain their original\nrecord and index, and each link carries the target node's lineage."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"treeLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects duplicate IDs, invalid parents, multiple roots, and\ncycles. Keep source order intentional because it controls child order when\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted. Collapsed branches remain application state; select the\nvisible rows before running the transform."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"left"},{"type":"text","value":" orientation anchors the root at the left and grows toward\nthe right. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"right"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom"},{"type":"text","value":" use the same stable tidy layout, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nodeSize"},{"type":"text","value":" controls semantic breadth and depth spacing. Normal scales own the\nresponsive mapping; resizing does not require a new hierarchy layout. Use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"},{"type":"text","value":" only when child order should differ from source order. Render links\nfirst, then nodes and labels. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dot and Hexagon Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-hierarchy-area","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare hierarchy area"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A treemap encodes each leaf's contribution as area while keeping leaves inside\ntheir parent branch. Use it when branch size matters more than exact depth or\nlink tracing."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"74-recharts-treemap","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The exact optional mark accepts the same flat path or parent-reference input as\nthe tidy-tree transform, but it owns final-pixel rectangles and labels:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { treemap } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/treemap'\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    treemap(rows, {\n      path: 'name',\n      delimiter: '.',\n      value: 'size',\n      ratio: 4 / 3,\n      round: true,\n      color: (node) => node.ancestorIds.at(-1) ?? node.id,\n      label: 'name',\n      inset: 1,\n      stroke: '#fff',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  guides: false,\n  margin: 0,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No x or y scale is configured. Squarification uses the final inner aspect ratio,\nso resizing may change which rectangles share an edge. Pixel padding and the\nscreen convention where y increases downward remain inside the mark."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default child order is the authored hierarchy order. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"},{"type":"text","value":" only when\nsibling order is a deliberate encoding. In-cell labels are centered and hidden\nwhen measured text plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelPadding"},{"type":"text","value":" does not fit. Color, label, state, and\npaint channels receive stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreemapNode"},{"type":"text","value":" values with hierarchy metadata,\naggregate value, the source row, and its original index. See the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/treemap.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Treemap Mark reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"partition-hierarchy-depth","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partition hierarchy depth"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sunburst uses angle for aggregate branch value and radius for hierarchy\ndepth. It preserves more depth structure than a treemap, but arc length is\nharder to compare precisely than aligned area or position."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the exact optional "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/sunburst.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sunburst"},{"type":"text","value":" mark"}]},{"type":"text","value":" inside\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polar"},{"type":"text","value":". It accepts the same path or explicit parent-reference hierarchy input\nas the other hierarchy entries, aggregates values, and allocates its sectors\nafter the final polar radius resolves. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"branchId"},{"type":"text","value":" for inherited branch\ncolor and direct "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SunburstNode"},{"type":"text","value":" lineage for tooltips and state callbacks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a large hierarchy, keep the complete source data but show only the next\none or two levels below a controlled root."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"126-drillable-sunburst","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rootId"},{"type":"text","value":" to the selected node and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"visibleDepth"},{"type":"text","value":" to the number of descendant\nrings. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSelect"},{"type":"text","value":" receives the same semantic node for pointer and keyboard\nactivation. The application owns the selected root and back control; the mark\nretains aggregate values and stable node keys. With the motion renderer,\nshared descendants interpolate their angles and radii while remaining centered\nsectors. Newly revealed nodes unfold from their disappearing parent sector,\nand drill-up reverses that relationship."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reveal-spatial-adjacency","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reveal spatial adjacency"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Delaunay network connects points that are neighbors in a triangulation. It\nanswers local spatial adjacency; it does not imply a business or causal\nrelationship unless the data model defines one."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"37-delaunay-network","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optional "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/delaunay.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delaunayLink"},{"type":"text","value":" mark"}]},{"type":"text","value":" accepts the\nsource points directly. It projects both configured axes after final layout,\ntriangulates each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" group in screen space, and retains both endpoint records\non every native link. Supply a stable point "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":"; edge identity is derived\nfrom the endpoint keys."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When Delaunay is used only for nearest-point lookup, keep the triangulation in\na "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpatialIndexFactory"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of painting its edges. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"explore-an-unconstrained-character-network","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explore an unconstrained character network"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A force-directed layout can reveal clusters and bridges when positions are not\nalready meaningful. It also introduces motion, stochastic initialization, and\ncollision policy that can make comparison unstable."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"40-force-directed-network","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the exact optional transform for a settled static network:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, dot, link, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { forceLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/network/force'\nimport { scaleLinear } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst graph = forceLayout(nodes, links, {\n  nodeKey: 'id',\n  source: 'source',\n  target: 'target',\n  iterations: 300,\n  forces: [\n    { type: 'link', distance: 42 },\n    { type: 'manyBody', strength: -120 },\n    { type: 'center' },\n    { type: 'collide', radius: 9 },\n  ],\n})\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    link(graph.links, {\n      x1: 'x1',\n      y1: 'y1',\n      x2: 'x2',\n      y2: 'y2',\n      key: ({ source, target }) => `${source}->${target}`,\n    }),\n    dot(graph.nodes, { x: 'x', y: 'y', color: 'group', key: 'id' }),\n    text(graph.nodes, { x: 'x', y: 'y', text: 'id', key: 'id' }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain(graph.xDomain) },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear().domain(graph.yDomain) },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forceLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" clones both inputs, applies the explicit forces in authored\norder, runs a fixed number of synchronous ticks, resolves link endpoints, and\nreturns padded domains. It is chart-size independent: resizing remaps the\nsettled coordinates and does not require another simulation. Keep node and\nlink order stable when comparisons must repeat exactly, and memoize the\ntransform when unchanged data would otherwise rebuild it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forceLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" is not a live simulation controller. If drag-to-reposition is\npart of the product, run the live controller outside the chart, store its\npositions in application state, and provide a keyboard-accessible alternative\nor detail control. The chart scene remains a projection of that controlled\nstate."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"labels-direction-and-weight","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Labels, direction, and weight"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use arrowheads only for genuinely directed edges."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Encode link weight sparingly; wide overlapping links can hide nodes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Label selected or important nodes instead of every node in a dense graph."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use color for stable semantic groups, not whichever cluster happens to be\nnear another after a simulation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide a searchable list or details panel for nodes that cannot be labeled\ndirectly."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom link paths or non-cartesian layouts may need a public custom mark. Start\nwith built-in links, dots, and text, then use\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Marks and Renderers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" only for\ngeometry that composition cannot express."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirm that links represent a documented relationship."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bound node and edge counts or aggregate the graph before rendering."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep node and edge IDs stable across revisions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Make layout initialization and ordering deterministic when comparison\nmatters."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Test disconnected nodes, cycles, missing parents, duplicate edges, and empty\ngraphs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not rely on color or pointer hover as the only identification path."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve keyboard focus and selection after layout updates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measure dense cases with "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/large-data.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large Data"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-topology","text":"Choose the topology","level":2},{"id":"start-with-a-basic-sankey","text":"Start with a basic Sankey","level":2},{"id":"customize-a-sankey","text":"Customize a Sankey","level":2},{"id":"show-a-strict-hierarchy","text":"Show a strict hierarchy","level":2},{"id":"compare-hierarchy-area","text":"Compare hierarchy area","level":2},{"id":"partition-hierarchy-depth","text":"Partition hierarchy depth","level":2},{"id":"reveal-spatial-adjacency","text":"Reveal spatial adjacency","level":2},{"id":"explore-an-unconstrained-character-network","text":"Explore an unconstrained character network","level":2},{"id":"labels-direction-and-weight","text":"Labels, direction, and weight","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nNetwork and hierarchy charts show relationships rather than values on two\nindependent quantitative axes. Node-link layouts can produce semantic\ncoordinates for ordinary marks. Area layouts such as treemaps and radial\npartitions such as sunbursts, and weighted flows such as Sankey diagrams,\ninstead depend on final layout bounds and render through responsive composite\nmarks.\n\nUse these views only when topology is the question. Dense networks quickly\nbecome less legible than a matrix, grouped summary, or searchable table.\n\n## Choose the topology\n\n| Reader question                                                | Start with                             |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| What is the parent-child structure and depth?                  | Tidy hierarchy tree                    |\n| Which positioned observations are spatial neighbors?           | Delaunay adjacency network             |\n| Which dependency clusters emerge without fixed positions?      | Force-directed network                 |\n| How does quantity split and recombine?                         | Basic Sankey                           |\n| How does value move through staged subtotals?                  | Sankey flow diagram                    |\n| How large are branches within a strict hierarchy?              | Treemap                                |\n| How does branch value divide across hierarchy depth?           | Sunburst                               |\n| Must many entities be compared by attributes, not connections? | A table, facets, or quantitative chart |\n\nLayout, traversal, grouping, and collision handling belong to eager data\npreparation unless they depend on final chart bounds. TanStack Charts provides\noptional static tree and force transforms plus final-layout spatial, hierarchy,\nand Sankey marks. [Scales and D3](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) documents that\nboundary.\n\n## Start with a basic Sankey\n\nThe smallest useful Sankey shows a single input splitting into two paths and\nrecombining into one output. Link width is the only quantitative encoding in\nthis example; nodes and links use the chart theme, and every node gets one\nshort name.\n\nUse this version as the starting point when the structure matters more than\nstyling. Its four explicit links preserve a total flow of 10 through a 60/40\nsplit.\n\nThe definition supplies semantic rows and composes ordinary marks after the\nresponsive layout resolves:\n\n```ts group=basic-sankey env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, link, rect, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { sankeyDiagram } from '@tanstack/charts/network/sankey'\nimport { links, nodes } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    sankeyDiagram({\n      nodes,\n      links,\n      nodeKey: 'id',\n      source: 'source',\n      target: 'target',\n      value: 'value',\n      align: 'left',\n      nodePadding: 28,\n      inset: { left: 16, right: 16, top: 24, bottom: 12 },\n      marks: ({ nodes: layoutNodes, links: layoutLinks }) =>\n        [\n          link(layoutLinks, {\n            x1: 'x1',\n            y1: 'y1',\n            x2: 'x2',\n            y2: 'y2',\n            key: 'key',\n            strokeWidth: (flow) => flow.width,\n          }),\n          rect(layoutNodes, {\n            x1: 'x0',\n            x2: 'x1',\n            y1: 'y0',\n            y2: 'y1',\n            key: 'key',\n            inset: 0,\n          }),\n          text(layoutNodes, {\n            x: 'x',\n            y: (node) => node.y0 - 8,\n            text: (node) => node.data.label,\n            key: 'key',\n            fill: 'currentColor',\n            fontSize: 12,\n            fontWeight: 650,\n          }),\n        ] as const,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  guides: false,\n  margin: 0,\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=basic-sankey file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const nodes = [\n  { id: 'input', label: 'Input' },\n  { id: 'path-a', label: 'Path A' },\n  { id: 'path-b', label: 'Path B' },\n  { id: 'output', label: 'Output' },\n]\n\nexport const links = [\n  { source: 'input', target: 'path-a', value: 6 },\n  { source: 'input', target: 'path-b', value: 4 },\n  { source: 'path-a', target: 'output', value: 6 },\n  { source: 'path-b', target: 'output', value: 4 },\n]\n```\n\n[Open the interactive basic Sankey catalog example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/111-basic-sankey/).\n\n## Customize a Sankey\n\nA Sankey diagram makes conservation and decomposition visible at the same\ntime: link width carries quantity, while each node marks a meaningful subtotal\nor outcome. This Apple FY22 income statement follows product and service\nrevenue through gross profit, operating costs, operating profit, and net\nprofit.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=111-sankey-flow height=500 -->\n\n`sankeyDiagram` owns the responsive flow layout, D3 mutation isolation,\nendpoint resolution, proportional widths, identity, and source lineage. Its\n`marks` callback keeps the income statement's authored order, compact wording,\nlabel side, two-line values, backdrops, title, and semantic colors beside the\nnative `link`, `rect`, and `text` definitions. The application does not import\n`d3-sankey`; the exact Charts subpath keeps it out of unrelated consumers.\n\nKeep every intermediate subtotal balanced. Use direct labels and tone as well\nas color so profit and cost paths remain identifiable. See the\n[Sankey diagram reference](../reference/marks/sankey.md) for responsive layout\noptions and immutable node/link fields.\n\n## Show a strict hierarchy\n\nA tidy tree assigns one position per node and one link per parent-child\nrelationship. Direct labels make a small hierarchy readable without requiring\nhover.\n\nUse the exact optional transform for a static tidy tree:\n\n```ts group=hierarchy-tree env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, dot, link, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { treeLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nconst hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\n  path: 'name',\n  delimiter: '.',\n})\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    link(hierarchy.links, {\n      x1: 'x1',\n      y1: 'y1',\n      x2: 'x2',\n      y2: 'y2',\n      key: 'id',\n      stroke: '#94a3b8',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    dot(hierarchy.nodes, {\n      x: 'x',\n      y: 'y',\n      key: 'id',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      r: 4,\n    }),\n    text(hierarchy.nodes, {\n      x: 'x',\n      y: 'y',\n      text: 'name',\n      key: 'id',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      anchor: (node) => (node.internal ? 'end' : 'start'),\n      dx: (node) => (node.internal ? -7 : 7),\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  guides: false,\n  margin: { top: 24, right: 110, bottom: 24, left: 64 },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=hierarchy-tree file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { name: 'Product' },\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics' },\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics.Reports' },\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics.Dashboards' },\n  { name: 'Product.Platform' },\n  { name: 'Product.Platform.API' },\n  { name: 'Product.Platform.Workers' },\n]\n```\n\n[Open the larger Flare hierarchy catalog example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/36-hierarchy-tree/).\n\nUse `id` and `parentId` instead of `path` for explicit parent-reference rows.\nPath input may omit ancestors; the result includes those structural nodes with\n`data: null` and empty source lineage. Explicit rows retain their original\nrecord and index, and each link carries the target node's lineage.\n\n`treeLayout` rejects duplicate IDs, invalid parents, multiple roots, and\ncycles. Keep source order intentional because it controls child order when\n`sort` is omitted. Collapsed branches remain application state; select the\nvisible rows before running the transform.\n\nThe default `left` orientation anchors the root at the left and grows toward\nthe right. `right`, `top`, and `bottom` use the same stable tidy layout, and\n`nodeSize` controls semantic breadth and depth spacing. Normal scales own the\nresponsive mapping; resizing does not require a new hierarchy layout. Use\n`sort` only when child order should differ from source order. Render links\nfirst, then nodes and labels. See\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md)\nand [Dot and Hexagon Marks](../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md).\n\n## Compare hierarchy area\n\nA treemap encodes each leaf's contribution as area while keeping leaves inside\ntheir parent branch. Use it when branch size matters more than exact depth or\nlink tracing.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=74-recharts-treemap height=480 -->\n\nThe exact optional mark accepts the same flat path or parent-reference input as\nthe tidy-tree transform, but it owns final-pixel rectangles and labels:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { treemap } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/treemap'\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    treemap(rows, {\n      path: 'name',\n      delimiter: '.',\n      value: 'size',\n      ratio: 4 / 3,\n      round: true,\n      color: (node) => node.ancestorIds.at(-1) ?? node.id,\n      label: 'name',\n      inset: 1,\n      stroke: '#fff',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  guides: false,\n  margin: 0,\n})\n```\n\nNo x or y scale is configured. Squarification uses the final inner aspect ratio,\nso resizing may change which rectangles share an edge. Pixel padding and the\nscreen convention where y increases downward remain inside the mark.\n\nThe default child order is the authored hierarchy order. Use `sort` only when\nsibling order is a deliberate encoding. In-cell labels are centered and hidden\nwhen measured text plus `labelPadding` does not fit. Color, label, state, and\npaint channels receive stable `TreemapNode` values with hierarchy metadata,\naggregate value, the source row, and its original index. See the\n[Treemap Mark reference](../reference/marks/treemap.md).\n\n## Partition hierarchy depth\n\nA sunburst uses angle for aggregate branch value and radius for hierarchy\ndepth. It preserves more depth structure than a treemap, but arc length is\nharder to compare precisely than aligned area or position.\n\nUse the exact optional [`sunburst` mark](../reference/marks/sunburst.md) inside\n`polar`. It accepts the same path or explicit parent-reference hierarchy input\nas the other hierarchy entries, aggregates values, and allocates its sectors\nafter the final polar radius resolves. Use `branchId` for inherited branch\ncolor and direct `SunburstNode` lineage for tooltips and state callbacks.\n\nFor a large hierarchy, keep the complete source data but show only the next\none or two levels below a controlled root.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=126-drillable-sunburst height=480 -->\n\nSet `rootId` to the selected node and `visibleDepth` to the number of descendant\nrings. `onSelect` receives the same semantic node for pointer and keyboard\nactivation. The application owns the selected root and back control; the mark\nretains aggregate values and stable node keys. With the motion renderer,\nshared descendants interpolate their angles and radii while remaining centered\nsectors. Newly revealed nodes unfold from their disappearing parent sector,\nand drill-up reverses that relationship.\n\n## Reveal spatial adjacency\n\nA Delaunay network connects points that are neighbors in a triangulation. It\nanswers local spatial adjacency; it does not imply a business or causal\nrelationship unless the data model defines one.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=37-delaunay-network height=480 -->\n\nThe optional [`delaunayLink` mark](../reference/marks/delaunay.md) accepts the\nsource points directly. It projects both configured axes after final layout,\ntriangulates each `z` group in screen space, and retains both endpoint records\non every native link. Supply a stable point `key`; edge identity is derived\nfrom the endpoint keys.\n\nWhen Delaunay is used only for nearest-point lookup, keep the triangulation in\na `ChartSpatialIndexFactory` instead of painting its edges. See\n[Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md).\n\n## Explore an unconstrained character network\n\nA force-directed layout can reveal clusters and bridges when positions are not\nalready meaningful. It also introduces motion, stochastic initialization, and\ncollision policy that can make comparison unstable.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=40-force-directed-network height=480 -->\n\nUse the exact optional transform for a settled static network:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, dot, link, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { forceLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/network/force'\nimport { scaleLinear } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst graph = forceLayout(nodes, links, {\n  nodeKey: 'id',\n  source: 'source',\n  target: 'target',\n  iterations: 300,\n  forces: [\n    { type: 'link', distance: 42 },\n    { type: 'manyBody', strength: -120 },\n    { type: 'center' },\n    { type: 'collide', radius: 9 },\n  ],\n})\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    link(graph.links, {\n      x1: 'x1',\n      y1: 'y1',\n      x2: 'x2',\n      y2: 'y2',\n      key: ({ source, target }) => `${source}->${target}`,\n    }),\n    dot(graph.nodes, { x: 'x', y: 'y', color: 'group', key: 'id' }),\n    text(graph.nodes, { x: 'x', y: 'y', text: 'id', key: 'id' }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain(graph.xDomain) },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear().domain(graph.yDomain) },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`forceLayout` clones both inputs, applies the explicit forces in authored\norder, runs a fixed number of synchronous ticks, resolves link endpoints, and\nreturns padded domains. It is chart-size independent: resizing remaps the\nsettled coordinates and does not require another simulation. Keep node and\nlink order stable when comparisons must repeat exactly, and memoize the\ntransform when unchanged data would otherwise rebuild it.\n\n`forceLayout` is not a live simulation controller. If drag-to-reposition is\npart of the product, run the live controller outside the chart, store its\npositions in application state, and provide a keyboard-accessible alternative\nor detail control. The chart scene remains a projection of that controlled\nstate.\n\n## Labels, direction, and weight\n\n- Use arrowheads only for genuinely directed edges.\n- Encode link weight sparingly; wide overlapping links can hide nodes.\n- Label selected or important nodes instead of every node in a dense graph.\n- Use color for stable semantic groups, not whichever cluster happens to be\n  near another after a simulation.\n- Provide a searchable list or details panel for nodes that cannot be labeled\n  directly.\n\nCustom link paths or non-cartesian layouts may need a public custom mark. Start\nwith built-in links, dots, and text, then use\n[Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md) only for\ngeometry that composition cannot express.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Confirm that links represent a documented relationship.\n- Bound node and edge counts or aggregate the graph before rendering.\n- Keep node and edge IDs stable across revisions.\n- Make layout initialization and ordering deterministic when comparison\n  matters.\n- Test disconnected nodes, cycles, missing parents, duplicate edges, and empty\n  graphs.\n- Do not rely on color or pointer hover as the only identification path.\n- Preserve keyboard focus and selection after layout updates.\n- Measure dense cases with [Large Data](../guides/large-data.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Networks and Hierarchies\ndescription: Choose tidy trees, Sankey flows, spatial adjacency graphs, and force-directed networks for connected or nested data.\n---\n\nNetwork and hierarchy charts show relationships rather than values on two\nindependent quantitative axes. Node-link layouts can produce semantic\ncoordinates for ordinary marks. Area layouts such as treemaps and radial\npartitions such as sunbursts, and weighted flows such as Sankey diagrams,\ninstead depend on final layout bounds and render through responsive composite\nmarks.\n\nUse these views only when topology is the question. Dense networks quickly\nbecome less legible than a matrix, grouped summary, or searchable table.\n\n## Choose the topology\n\n| Reader question                                                | Start with                             |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| What is the parent-child structure and depth?                  | Tidy hierarchy tree                    |\n| Which positioned observations are spatial neighbors?           | Delaunay adjacency network             |\n| Which dependency clusters emerge without fixed positions?      | Force-directed network                 |\n| How does quantity split and recombine?                         | Basic Sankey                           |\n| How does value move through staged subtotals?                  | Sankey flow diagram                    |\n| How large are branches within a strict hierarchy?              | Treemap                                |\n| How does branch value divide across hierarchy depth?           | Sunburst                               |\n| Must many entities be compared by attributes, not connections? | A table, facets, or quantitative chart |\n\nLayout, traversal, grouping, and collision handling belong to eager data\npreparation unless they depend on final chart bounds. TanStack Charts provides\noptional static tree and force transforms plus final-layout spatial, hierarchy,\nand Sankey marks. [Scales and D3](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) documents that\nboundary.\n\n## Start with a basic Sankey\n\nThe smallest useful Sankey shows a single input splitting into two paths and\nrecombining into one output. Link width is the only quantitative encoding in\nthis example; nodes and links use the chart theme, and every node gets one\nshort name.\n\nUse this version as the starting point when the structure matters more than\nstyling. Its four explicit links preserve a total flow of 10 through a 60/40\nsplit.\n\nThe definition supplies semantic rows and composes ordinary marks after the\nresponsive layout resolves:\n\n```ts group=basic-sankey env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, link, rect, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { sankeyDiagram } from '@tanstack/charts/network/sankey'\nimport { links, nodes } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    sankeyDiagram({\n      nodes,\n      links,\n      nodeKey: 'id',\n      source: 'source',\n      target: 'target',\n      value: 'value',\n      align: 'left',\n      nodePadding: 28,\n      inset: { left: 16, right: 16, top: 24, bottom: 12 },\n      marks: ({ nodes: layoutNodes, links: layoutLinks }) =>\n        [\n          link(layoutLinks, {\n            x1: 'x1',\n            y1: 'y1',\n            x2: 'x2',\n            y2: 'y2',\n            key: 'key',\n            strokeWidth: (flow) => flow.width,\n          }),\n          rect(layoutNodes, {\n            x1: 'x0',\n            x2: 'x1',\n            y1: 'y0',\n            y2: 'y1',\n            key: 'key',\n            inset: 0,\n          }),\n          text(layoutNodes, {\n            x: 'x',\n            y: (node) => node.y0 - 8,\n            text: (node) => node.data.label,\n            key: 'key',\n            fill: 'currentColor',\n            fontSize: 12,\n            fontWeight: 650,\n          }),\n        ] as const,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  guides: false,\n  margin: 0,\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=basic-sankey file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const nodes = [\n  { id: 'input', label: 'Input' },\n  { id: 'path-a', label: 'Path A' },\n  { id: 'path-b', label: 'Path B' },\n  { id: 'output', label: 'Output' },\n]\n\nexport const links = [\n  { source: 'input', target: 'path-a', value: 6 },\n  { source: 'input', target: 'path-b', value: 4 },\n  { source: 'path-a', target: 'output', value: 6 },\n  { source: 'path-b', target: 'output', value: 4 },\n]\n```\n\n[Open the interactive basic Sankey catalog example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/111-basic-sankey/).\n\n## Customize a Sankey\n\nA Sankey diagram makes conservation and decomposition visible at the same\ntime: link width carries quantity, while each node marks a meaningful subtotal\nor outcome. This Apple FY22 income statement follows product and service\nrevenue through gross profit, operating costs, operating profit, and net\nprofit.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=111-sankey-flow height=500 -->\n\n`sankeyDiagram` owns the responsive flow layout, D3 mutation isolation,\nendpoint resolution, proportional widths, identity, and source lineage. Its\n`marks` callback keeps the income statement's authored order, compact wording,\nlabel side, two-line values, backdrops, title, and semantic colors beside the\nnative `link`, `rect`, and `text` definitions. The application does not import\n`d3-sankey`; the exact Charts subpath keeps it out of unrelated consumers.\n\nKeep every intermediate subtotal balanced. Use direct labels and tone as well\nas color so profit and cost paths remain identifiable. See the\n[Sankey diagram reference](../reference/marks/sankey.md) for responsive layout\noptions and immutable node/link fields.\n\n## Show a strict hierarchy\n\nA tidy tree assigns one position per node and one link per parent-child\nrelationship. Direct labels make a small hierarchy readable without requiring\nhover.\n\nUse the exact optional transform for a static tidy tree:\n\n```ts group=hierarchy-tree env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, dot, link, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { treeLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nconst hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\n  path: 'name',\n  delimiter: '.',\n})\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    link(hierarchy.links, {\n      x1: 'x1',\n      y1: 'y1',\n      x2: 'x2',\n      y2: 'y2',\n      key: 'id',\n      stroke: '#94a3b8',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    dot(hierarchy.nodes, {\n      x: 'x',\n      y: 'y',\n      key: 'id',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      r: 4,\n    }),\n    text(hierarchy.nodes, {\n      x: 'x',\n      y: 'y',\n      text: 'name',\n      key: 'id',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      anchor: (node) => (node.internal ? 'end' : 'start'),\n      dx: (node) => (node.internal ? -7 : 7),\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  guides: false,\n  margin: { top: 24, right: 110, bottom: 24, left: 64 },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=hierarchy-tree file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { name: 'Product' },\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics' },\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics.Reports' },\n  { name: 'Product.Analytics.Dashboards' },\n  { name: 'Product.Platform' },\n  { name: 'Product.Platform.API' },\n  { name: 'Product.Platform.Workers' },\n]\n```\n\n[Open the larger Flare hierarchy catalog example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/36-hierarchy-tree/).\n\nUse `id` and `parentId` instead of `path` for explicit parent-reference rows.\nPath input may omit ancestors; the result includes those structural nodes with\n`data: null` and empty source lineage. Explicit rows retain their original\nrecord and index, and each link carries the target node's lineage.\n\n`treeLayout` rejects duplicate IDs, invalid parents, multiple roots, and\ncycles. Keep source order intentional because it controls child order when\n`sort` is omitted. Collapsed branches remain application state; select the\nvisible rows before running the transform.\n\nThe default `left` orientation anchors the root at the left and grows toward\nthe right. `right`, `top`, and `bottom` use the same stable tidy layout, and\n`nodeSize` controls semantic breadth and depth spacing. Normal scales own the\nresponsive mapping; resizing does not require a new hierarchy layout. Use\n`sort` only when child order should differ from source order. Render links\nfirst, then nodes and labels. See\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md)\nand [Dot and Hexagon Marks](../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md).\n\n## Compare hierarchy area\n\nA treemap encodes each leaf's contribution as area while keeping leaves inside\ntheir parent branch. Use it when branch size matters more than exact depth or\nlink tracing.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=74-recharts-treemap height=480 -->\n\nThe exact optional mark accepts the same flat path or parent-reference input as\nthe tidy-tree transform, but it owns final-pixel rectangles and labels:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { treemap } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/treemap'\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    treemap(rows, {\n      path: 'name',\n      delimiter: '.',\n      value: 'size',\n      ratio: 4 / 3,\n      round: true,\n      color: (node) => node.ancestorIds.at(-1) ?? node.id,\n      label: 'name',\n      inset: 1,\n      stroke: '#fff',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  guides: false,\n  margin: 0,\n})\n```\n\nNo x or y scale is configured. Squarification uses the final inner aspect ratio,\nso resizing may change which rectangles share an edge. Pixel padding and the\nscreen convention where y increases downward remain inside the mark.\n\nThe default child order is the authored hierarchy order. Use `sort` only when\nsibling order is a deliberate encoding. In-cell labels are centered and hidden\nwhen measured text plus `labelPadding` does not fit. Color, label, state, and\npaint channels receive stable `TreemapNode` values with hierarchy metadata,\naggregate value, the source row, and its original index. See the\n[Treemap Mark reference](../reference/marks/treemap.md).\n\n## Partition hierarchy depth\n\nA sunburst uses angle for aggregate branch value and radius for hierarchy\ndepth. It preserves more depth structure than a treemap, but arc length is\nharder to compare precisely than aligned area or position.\n\nUse the exact optional [`sunburst` mark](../reference/marks/sunburst.md) inside\n`polar`. It accepts the same path or explicit parent-reference hierarchy input\nas the other hierarchy entries, aggregates values, and allocates its sectors\nafter the final polar radius resolves. Use `branchId` for inherited branch\ncolor and direct `SunburstNode` lineage for tooltips and state callbacks.\n\nFor a large hierarchy, keep the complete source data but show only the next\none or two levels below a controlled root.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=126-drillable-sunburst height=480 -->\n\nSet `rootId` to the selected node and `visibleDepth` to the number of descendant\nrings. `onSelect` receives the same semantic node for pointer and keyboard\nactivation. The application owns the selected root and back control; the mark\nretains aggregate values and stable node keys. With the motion renderer,\nshared descendants interpolate their angles and radii while remaining centered\nsectors. Newly revealed nodes unfold from their disappearing parent sector,\nand drill-up reverses that relationship.\n\n## Reveal spatial adjacency\n\nA Delaunay network connects points that are neighbors in a triangulation. It\nanswers local spatial adjacency; it does not imply a business or causal\nrelationship unless the data model defines one.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=37-delaunay-network height=480 -->\n\nThe optional [`delaunayLink` mark](../reference/marks/delaunay.md) accepts the\nsource points directly. It projects both configured axes after final layout,\ntriangulates each `z` group in screen space, and retains both endpoint records\non every native link. Supply a stable point `key`; edge identity is derived\nfrom the endpoint keys.\n\nWhen Delaunay is used only for nearest-point lookup, keep the triangulation in\na `ChartSpatialIndexFactory` instead of painting its edges. See\n[Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md).\n\n## Explore an unconstrained character network\n\nA force-directed layout can reveal clusters and bridges when positions are not\nalready meaningful. It also introduces motion, stochastic initialization, and\ncollision policy that can make comparison unstable.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=40-force-directed-network height=480 -->\n\nUse the exact optional transform for a settled static network:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, dot, link, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { forceLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/network/force'\nimport { scaleLinear } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst graph = forceLayout(nodes, links, {\n  nodeKey: 'id',\n  source: 'source',\n  target: 'target',\n  iterations: 300,\n  forces: [\n    { type: 'link', distance: 42 },\n    { type: 'manyBody', strength: -120 },\n    { type: 'center' },\n    { type: 'collide', radius: 9 },\n  ],\n})\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    link(graph.links, {\n      x1: 'x1',\n      y1: 'y1',\n      x2: 'x2',\n      y2: 'y2',\n      key: ({ source, target }) => `${source}->${target}`,\n    }),\n    dot(graph.nodes, { x: 'x', y: 'y', color: 'group', key: 'id' }),\n    text(graph.nodes, { x: 'x', y: 'y', text: 'id', key: 'id' }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain(graph.xDomain) },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear().domain(graph.yDomain) },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`forceLayout` clones both inputs, applies the explicit forces in authored\norder, runs a fixed number of synchronous ticks, resolves link endpoints, and\nreturns padded domains. It is chart-size independent: resizing remaps the\nsettled coordinates and does not require another simulation. Keep node and\nlink order stable when comparisons must repeat exactly, and memoize the\ntransform when unchanged data would otherwise rebuild it.\n\n`forceLayout` is not a live simulation controller. If drag-to-reposition is\npart of the product, run the live controller outside the chart, store its\npositions in application state, and provide a keyboard-accessible alternative\nor detail control. The chart scene remains a projection of that controlled\nstate.\n\n## Labels, direction, and weight\n\n- Use arrowheads only for genuinely directed edges.\n- Encode link weight sparingly; wide overlapping links can hide nodes.\n- Label selected or important nodes instead of every node in a dense graph.\n- Use color for stable semantic groups, not whichever cluster happens to be\n  near another after a simulation.\n- Provide a searchable list or details panel for nodes that cannot be labeled\n  directly.\n\nCustom link paths or non-cartesian layouts may need a public custom mark. Start\nwith built-in links, dots, and text, then use\n[Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md) only for\ngeometry that composition cannot express.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Confirm that links represent a documented relationship.\n- Bound node and edge counts or aggregate the graph before rendering.\n- Keep node and edge IDs stable across revisions.\n- Make layout initialization and ordering deterministic when comparison\n  matters.\n- Test disconnected nodes, cycles, missing parents, duplicate edges, and empty\n  graphs.\n- Do not rely on color or pointer hover as the only identification path.\n- Preserve keyboard focus and selection after layout updates.\n- Measure dense cases with [Large Data](../guides/large-data.md).\n"}
